---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Dunhuang vs Jiuquan: Which Silk Road City Should You Visit? (2026)"
  description="Honest comparison of Dunhuang and Jiuquan for Silk Road travelers: attractions, costs, food, accessibility, best time to visit, and how to split your time between both cities."
>

  <article class="pt-24 pb-24">
    <div class="section-container max-w-3xl">
      <header class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tighter leading-[1.05] text-stone-900">
          Dunhuang vs Jiuquan
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
          An honest, practical comparison for Silk Road travelers. Which city deserves more of your time? How should you split your itinerary? Answered with real traveler priorities.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Full Comparison Table -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 mb-8">Full Comparison</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-stone-200">
          <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead class="bg-stone-100">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 w-1/4"></th>
                <th class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-mogao-600">Dunhuang</th>
                <th class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-600">Jiuquan</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100">
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Vibe</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Ancient Silk Road oasis, cultural melting pot, backpacker hub</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Frontier fortress, space city, fewer tourists, more local</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Signature Attraction</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Mogao Caves (UNESCO, Buddhist cave art)</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Jiayuguan Fort (Ming Dynasty Great Wall terminus)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Other Highlights</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Crescent Lake, Singing Sand Dunes, Yadan Geopark, Yumen Pass</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Overhanging Great Wall, Satellite Launch Center, Wei-Jin Tombs</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Ideal Stay</td><td class="px-6 py-4">2-3 days minimum</td><td class="px-6 py-4">1-2 days</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Daily Budget</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$50-200 (wide range, from hostels to boutique)</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$40-150 (generally cheaper)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Hotel Quality</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Excellent range, international brands present</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Limited; better to stay in Jiayuguan (20km away)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Food Scene</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Vibrant night market, diverse Silk Road cuisine</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Solid local food, lamb specialties, fewer international options</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">English-Friendliness</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Good at major sites and hotels</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Limited; Chinese helpful</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Tourist Crowds</td><td class="px-6 py-4">High in peak season (especially Chinese domestic tourists)</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Moderate; noticeably quieter than Dunhuang</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Best Time</td><td class="px-6 py-4">May-June, September-October</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Same as Dunhuang; July-August is hot</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold text-stone-900 bg-stone-50">Internet & Payment</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Good WiFi at most hotels; mobile payment widely accepted</td><td class="px-6 py-4">WiFi can be spotty; cash still useful at small vendors</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- How to Split Your Time -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 mb-6">How to Split Your Time</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-stone-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-stone-200">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900">You have 3 days</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-stone-600">Allocate all 3 days to Dunhuang. Trying to squeeze in Jiuquan with only 3 days means you rush both cities and enjoy neither. Dunhuang has more must-see attractions and is the stronger stand-alone destination.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-900 font-semibold">Split: Dunhuang 3 | Jiuquan 0</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-stone-200">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900">You have 5 days</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-stone-600">Spend 3 days in Dunhuang (Mogao Caves, Crescent Lake, Yadan Geopark) and then take the 2.5-hour high-speed train east. Spend 1 day at Jiayuguan Fort and 1 day exploring Jiuquan city, including the satellite center exhibit.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-900 font-semibold">Split: Dunhuang 3 | Jiuquan 2</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-stone-200">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900">You have 7+ days</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-stone-600">The sweet spot. Spend 3 full days in Dunhuang, 2 days in Jiuquan/Jiayuguan, and use the remaining 2 days to add Zhangye (Rainbow Mountains, an easy 1.5-hour train from Jiuquan). This gives you the complete Gansu Corridor experience.</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-900 font-semibold">Split: Dunhuang 3 | Jiuquan 2 | Zhangye 2</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- By Traveler Type -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 mb-6">Which City Matches Your Style?</h2>
        <div class="space-y-5">
          <div class="bg-mogao-50/50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-mogao-200">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-mogao-700">History &amp; Art Lover</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600">Dunhuang is your city. The Mogao Caves alone are worth the trip. Add the Dunhuang Museum for deeper context on Silk Road history.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-100 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-stone-200">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-stone-700">Photographer</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600">Dunhuang wins for variety: desert dunes at sunrise, cave facades, and the surreal Yadan rock formations. Jiuquan offers dramatic Great Wall vistas and snow-capped Qilian Mountains as a backdrop.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-mogao-50/50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-mogao-200">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-mogao-700">Military History Buff</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600">Jiuquan is essential. Jiayuguan Fort is the westernmost fortress of the Ming Great Wall, and the Overhanging Great Wall gives you a real sense of the wall's defensive architecture.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-100 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-stone-200">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-stone-700">Space &amp; Science Enthusiast</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600">Jiuquan is unrivaled. The Jiuquan Satellite Launch Center is China's primary spaceport, having launched all of China's crewed missions. The visitor center offers a rare glimpse into China's space program.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-mogao-50/50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-mogao-200">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-mogao-700">Solo Budget Backpacker</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600">Dunhuang is more accommodating. Better hostel scene, more English-speaking travelers, and more shared-transport options. Jiuquan is doable but rougher around the edges for independent travel.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-stone-100 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-stone-200">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-stone-700">Off-the-Beaten-Path Seeker</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-stone-600">Jiuquan is far less touristy. If Dunhuang feels too polished, Jiuquan and the surrounding desert towns will feel more raw and real.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Bottom Line -->
      <section class="mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 mb-6">The Bottom Line</h2>
        <div class="bg-stone-900 text-stone-200 rounded-2xl p-8">
          <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
            If you can only visit one city, <strong class="text-white">pick Dunhuang</strong>. It is the cultural heart of the Silk Road and has more to offer the first-time visitor. But if you have 5 or more days, <strong class="text-white">do not skip Jiuquan</strong>. The combination of Jiayuguan Fort and China's space history makes it a genuinely unique destination that complements, rather than duplicates, the Dunhuang experience.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-stone-400 text-sm">
            The high-speed train between the two cities takes only 2.5 hours. There is no good reason not to visit both if your schedule allows it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="text-center">
        <a href="/#planner" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3.5 bg-mogao-600 text-white rounded-xl text-sm font-semibold hover:bg-mogao-700 transition-colors">
          Plan Your Itinerary Now
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/><polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"/></svg>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>      <section class="mt-16 p-6 bg-stone-50 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 reveal">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-display font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-3">Sources &amp; References</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600">
          <li>&rarr; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunhuang" rel="external nofollow" target="_blank" class="text-mogao-600 hover:text-mogao-700 underline">Dunhuang on Wikipedia</a> — city history and Silk Road context</li>
          <li>&rarr; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiuquan" rel="external nofollow" target="_blank" class="text-mogao-600 hover:text-mogao-700 underline">Jiuquan on Wikipedia</a> — city history and modern development</li>
          <li>&rarr; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexi_Corridor" rel="external nofollow" target="_blank" class="text-mogao-600 hover:text-mogao-700 underline">Hexi Corridor on Wikipedia</a> — historical trade route context</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
  
  </article>
</BaseLayout>


